WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:07.490
RETO MEIER: I'm Reto Meier.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.115
STEPHANIE WONG: And
I'm Stephanie Wong.

00:00:09.115 --> 00:00:11.560
Joining us now, Rae Wang,
Security Product Manager

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.570
for Google Cloud.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:13.820
Thanks so much for joining us.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:14.120
RAE WANG: Thank you.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:14.770
It's great to be here.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.395
STEPHANIE WONG: So, security is
such a vital part of everything

00:00:17.395 --> 00:00:18.970
we do online and in the cloud.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.860
Let's talk about
Security At Scale.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:24.110
As our environments grow larger,
security simply must keep up.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.780
How is network
security changing as we

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.310
move from brittle environments
built on static IPs,

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:29.740
to microservices?

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.368
RAE WANG: The traditional
IP-based networking policy

00:00:32.368 --> 00:00:34.160
is required to have
very detailed knowledge

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.290
on the nitty-gritty parts of
your networking configurations.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.870
They don't really offer you
a concept of which service

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:44.230
can talk to what, unless you
segregate them by IP addresses

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:45.850
and subnets ahead of time.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:47.980
And this quickly
becomes unwieldy as you

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.410
scale your infrastructure,
especially as you introduce

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.300
the concept of microservices.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.250
And this is where a
cloud-native platform

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.440
like SGO, Automanage,
version CSM,

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.230
give a very different
kind of experience.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.990
They allow the admins to
define high-level policies that

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.730
focus on application
requirements of business logic.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.230
The microservices are backed
by [INAUDIBLE],, which give you

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:11.770
a natural isolation
boundaries, and each service

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.330
is also given a
unique [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.830
of service identity.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.410
Then you can set policies
leveraging these identities

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.590
and boundaries.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:21.150
For example, you can set
to be zero trust by default

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.860
between the services, so that
any communication will require

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.860
explicit policies, and
then mutual TRS is enforced

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:30.370
by default. And once you
have configured the policies,

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:34.230
CSM can allow you to centrally
manage and monitor and control

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:37.760
Service-to-Service traffic,
and alert any policy violation.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.290
So this is how with a
microservice-focused platform

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.940
CSM, it's much
simpler to both create

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.702
and do ongoing monitoring of
network security policies.

00:01:46.702 --> 00:01:49.390
RETO MEIER: So how does moving
into a hybrid cloud environment

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:50.812
impact security?

00:01:50.812 --> 00:01:53.020
RAE WANG: So moving to hybrid
environment, especially

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.960
with protocol
Anthos, means you now

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.920
have a consistent control plane
between on-prem and cloud.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.170
It gives you a single
admin experience

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.320
in a single pane of glass, which
makes it much simpler to have

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.835
central visibility and control
across hybrid deployments.

00:02:08.835 --> 00:02:10.210
And then you can
also start using

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.820
some of the Google
Security capabilities

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.320
even for on-prem workloads.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.110
For example, products
like Binary Authorization

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:22.150
or Anthos Config Management
now work for on-prem as well.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.210
And, of course, another benefit
of using hybrid platform

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:28.270
is that helps your workloads
to get cloud ready,

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.720
because containers are portable
and the admin may experience

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:32.240
the same.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:33.760
So when it comes
time to migrate,

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.720
it's a very
straightforward process.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.050
And, of course, once your
workloads migrate to cloud,

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:41.860
we offer you additional security
values, things like [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.800
VMs or zero downtime patching
through light migration,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.040
even when there are really
big hardware vulnerabilities,

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.540
like Spectre and Meltdown.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.700
STEPHANIE WONG: You mentioned
security consistency.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.158
What are some of
the best practices

00:02:53.158 --> 00:02:55.450
to ensure a security
baseline with consistency

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.230
and reproducibility for all of
your resources in the cloud?

00:02:58.230 --> 00:02:59.320
RAE WANG: Great question.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.720
We know that security is kind
of a shared responsibility.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.200
We do our part and
customers do their parts.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.470
So, in a platform, we offer
a lot of security capability

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.570
so that customers can start
on a much higher baseline.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.210
But at the same time,
we also make sure

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:17.290
that your part of responsibility
becomes easy as well,

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:20.080
and that's often down through
templatized deployments,

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.330
central config and policy
management, and ongoing

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:23.780
monitoring and learning.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.640
So in terms of deployments,
by using community-style API,

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.950
you can express
your configurations

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:31.610
as declarative templates.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.090
Then Anthos Config
Management gives you

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.580
a central source of
truth for these templates

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.740
and allow you to deploy
the config and policy

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:42.280
updates by GitOps and
[INAUDIBLE] best practices.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:45.460
These changes then get
deployed to across RFA Clusters

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.240
and measures an environment
to ensure consistency.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.620
And when it comes to
constraints, a couple

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.935
frameworks, Gatekeeper, policy
controller, and facility config

00:03:54.935 --> 00:03:57.310
validators that we're actually
developing the open source

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:58.720
with communities.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.870
They allow admins to
standardize configurations

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:03.670
across your entire organization.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.015
You can, for example, set
guardrails by presubmit checks

00:04:07.015 --> 00:04:09.940
in your release pipeline so
that misconfiguration cannot get

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.570
into the deployment process.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:15.120
And then, in case drift
happens in production,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.000
you can also set alerts
so they can get notified.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.130
For monitoring, we also have
Cloud [INAUDIBLE] and Cloud

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.810
Security Command Center.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.240
They give you a central
view of the state

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.745
of RFA resources in
production and then

00:04:27.745 --> 00:04:30.120
can notify you of changes and
risks and security findings

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.460
as well.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:32.950
So, together, these
tools help you

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:36.830
through the lifecycle of
managing your security config

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:37.330
settings.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.680
RETO MEIER: That's fantastic.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.320
Can you talk a little bit
about declarative configs

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.100
to make security controls
consistent across scale

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.970
diverse environments.

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:50.680
RAE WANG: We all know one of the
most costly mistakes an admin

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.560
can make is misconfiguration.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.420
It's almost far too easy
to accidentally set your IM

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.500
controls to be to be public or
make your network configuration

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:00.745
to be way too permissive.

00:05:00.745 --> 00:05:03.120
And when you do that, you open
up your sensitive resource

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.950
to attacks and data leaks.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:09.933
So this is where the declarative
config makes a difference.

00:05:09.933 --> 00:05:12.100
Because it helps the admin
to focus on the end goal,

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.150
the end state.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:16.810
And then allow you to offload
the intermediate orchestration

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:19.120
and workflows and keeping
up consistencies, really,

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:20.680
to the platform.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.650
And then when we do that
across a lot of resources,

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:25.780
across many clusters
and measures,

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:29.200
we basically achieve
reproducibility and recovery

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:30.010
from drift.

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.160
And therefore they
reduce the complexity

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.095
of applying security controls.

00:05:34.095 --> 00:05:35.470
And they also make
it much easier

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:38.960
to share security best
practices because you can deploy

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.263
template solutions.

00:05:40.263 --> 00:05:41.680
STEPHANIE WONG:
And not only is it

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.055
important to
declare the security

00:05:43.055 --> 00:05:44.620
configs across
your environments,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.590
but also monitor
inevitable risks.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.340
So we have a big launch,
as you mentioned,

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:49.955
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:05:49.955 --> 00:05:51.580
How do we help
customers get visibility

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.315
into risk in their environments?

00:05:53.315 --> 00:05:55.690
RAE WANG: We're very excited
about Cloud Security Command

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:59.500
Center, because it kind of
unifies the security detections

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.930
from Google together with a
lot of the key third party

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.210
detections for the
main central place

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:06.850
and really help the admins
to build them, analyze them,

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:09.140
and take actions in place.

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:11.260
For example, one
of the capabilities

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.410
is security house analytics.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:17.260
It scans and detects over
20 common vulnerabilities,

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.630
from publicly exposed
storage buckets,

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:22.270
to talk about overly
permissive firewall rules,

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:25.170
to misconfigured Kubernetes
clusters, and so on.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:28.750
And similarly, you have
Cloud anomaly detection.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.960
It automatically detects
some of the key threats

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.230
from cryptojacking to
exposed service credentials,

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:36.850
to outbound DDoS.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:39.370
And then with this GA release,
another benefit you get

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:43.150
is now Cloud SEC unifies
security findings

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:45.880
from a variety of
Google Cloud products,

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.730
from event threat detection
to binary authorization,

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:52.990
to cloud asset inventory,
to [INAUDIBLE] and so on.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:55.755
How you can use it, well, if
you're in a [INAUDIBLE] company

00:06:55.755 --> 00:06:57.880
and you have a finance
department, for example that

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.090
stores credit card information
and sensitive personal data,

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:04.690
you can use Cloud SEC to
scan the infrastructure used

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.340
for storing these sensitive
information, identifying

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.940
your vulnerabilities, as well
as finding active threats that

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:11.732
are targeting these
vulnerabilities.

00:07:11.732 --> 00:07:13.190
And then there's
a lot of findings.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:16.570
So we give you a way to manage
and organize these findings

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:20.290
by attaching business context
to the findings and resources

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:22.780
by our code security marks.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.610
And then we all know
that getting the data

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:25.498
is the first step.

00:07:25.498 --> 00:07:28.040
What you really want to do is
to improve your security poster

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:28.880
validator.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:31.810
So Cloud SEC also allows you
to actually respond to take

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:35.200
actions by, for example,
triggering alerts or creating

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:38.830
[INAUDIBLE] incidents, or
directly executing remediation

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.282
operations by cloud functions, .

00:07:40.282 --> 00:07:41.740
STEPHANIE WONG:
Great, thanks, Rae.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:43.448
Your insights into
security will continue

00:07:43.448 --> 00:07:45.740
to be vital to any company
that wants to build and grow

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:46.240
quickly.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:47.410
RAE WANG: Thank you.

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:53.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

